{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>If his master gives him a wife.</b> At a time when he is already married, then specifically is his master permitted to give him a maidservant. Some say the reason is that if he is unmarried there is reason to fear lest he say, 'I love my wife' — the maidservant; but if he is already married to an Israelite woman there is not so much cause for concern, for his soul has already cleaved in love to the Israelite woman. And some say the reason is that if he is married, his master is liable for the food of his wife and children, and then no master would want to acquire him and thrust his head into this yoke; therefore, to counterbalance this, the Torah granted him the prerogative of being able to give him a maidservant, to beget slaves from her; but if he is unmarried he has many buyers without this. And perhaps there is yet another matter within it, on account of the nullification of the command to be fruitful and multiply: for if he is married, he is already fulfilling this commandment with the Israelite woman, and the Torah was not concerned about the involvement he would also have with the maidservant; but if he is unmarried, he would nullify himself from the commandment of being fruitful and multiplying, and would consume his seed with the disqualified maidservant."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:04.503051620Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}